00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.390
So let's start with
why Istio, right?

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.840
We've just finished kind
of getting into Kubernetes

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.410
and be comfortable there.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.830
And now we're going to add
another layer of abstraction

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:15.830
on top of it.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:17.220
Why do we want to
do this, right?

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.870
Why is Istio so critically
useful and important

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.396
to deployments?

00:02:22.396 --> 00:02:23.930
Right, we have containers.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.140
Containers are great.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.540
We love containers, right?

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.450
It lets us be really portable
and get out of this whole,

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.970
like, it worked on my
machine, but not over there

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:32.922
in production.

00:02:32.922 --> 00:02:34.880
So we can get this sort
of unified environment,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.440
get this great packaging.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.590
Containers, awesome.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.280
Kubernetes, also great, right?

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.825
It does a lot of
great things for us.

00:02:41.825 --> 00:02:43.700
It orchestrates those
containers really well.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.533
It lets us schedule
amount and bin pack them

00:02:45.533 --> 00:02:48.050
very tightly into a cluster.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.690
Really powerful.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.110
But microservices
deployments are hard,

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:56.450
by their just very
nature, right?

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.720
You have a bunch of services
strewn across a cluster.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.030
Teams are iterating
at different paces.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.780
You've got dependencies you
don't even know about, right?

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.040
Again, spread out
across this whole thing.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.650
Applying policy,
applying control

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.470
is challenging in that
environment, right?

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:15.560
Istio helps us get
from that sort of mess

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.690
on the left to something
that's more organized

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.027
and functional on the right.

00:03:21.027 --> 00:03:22.610
If you think about
some of the, again,

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:24.380
that higher level of
abstraction, right?

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.080
So we've moved on from
just containers, and pods,

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.838
and scheduling them and
deploying them into a cluster.

00:03:29.838 --> 00:03:31.880
But again, if you think
about that next level up,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.910
what makes those microservices
deployments hard, right?

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:38.640
So a good example is Canary
releases or doing blue green,

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.610
or traffic splitting.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.740
If you want to do that
in Kubernetes today,

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.950
you actually have to run
the correct ratio of pods.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:48.290
So if you wanted to do
something like a 90/10 split

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.290
to test out v1 versus
v2, you'd actually

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:55.340
have to run nine pods
of v1 and one pod of v2

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.110
to make sure you've
got that 90/10 split.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.990
That's kind of a pain, and
it requires a little too much

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.035
handholding to really work well.

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:06.160
You don't really know who
you're talking to, right?

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.022
Your services
could have a person

00:04:08.022 --> 00:04:09.980
in the middle or a service
in the middle there,

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.438
where you could be
opening yourself

00:04:11.438 --> 00:04:13.380
up to some kind of
attack or injection.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:14.080
Right?

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.360
You want to make
sure that services

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.029
know who they're communicating
with back and forth.

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:23.140
You want to be able to quickly
dig into high latency drivers.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.570
Again, if you think of a
microservices deployment,

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.820
you may have a web request
that comes in the frontend,

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.030
but it may travel around to
a number of other services.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.580
Well, how do you know
if it's slow, right?

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.500
The end user might
feel like, well, this

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.220
is taking 500 milliseconds
or over 1,000 milliseconds.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.640
But it might not
be the frontend.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.265
It might be one of
the various backends

00:04:42.265 --> 00:04:44.310
that that frontend
is talking to.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.310
And there's a whole host
of other things, right?

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.788
Like digging into L7 attributes,
like request headers.

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:51.955
We can't do that right out
of the box in Kubernetes.

00:04:51.955 --> 00:04:54.770
You can't really control
who accesses services,

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.150
and you can't track requests
from start to finish.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.000
So you don't have a
level of visibility

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.050
to match, again, the complexity
of the deployment you're doing.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.730
So that's a little
bit of why we think

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:06.605
Istio is really powerful
because it gives you

00:05:06.605 --> 00:05:09.500
access to all of these.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.507
So again, we've talked
about service mesh.

00:05:11.507 --> 00:05:13.340
Many of you obviously
have played with this.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.380
[INAUDIBLE] have some
understanding here.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:20.810
But I'd like to start with this
idea of what is Istio, right?

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.280
Istio, if you think of it
and if you boil it down

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.240
to its kind of
simplest concept, Istio

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.970
is about automation, right?

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:31.060
It goes beyond, I think, just
a sort of a service mesh.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:33.160
I don't think that
really encapsulates it.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.050
Istio gives you this power
to apply policy and control

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.240
to your entire
deployment, whether it's

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.060
five services or 500 services.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:46.840
You can take a single rule and
say, I want to turn on MTLS

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.480
and make every service mutually
authenticate to each other

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:50.590
with one rule.

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:52.090
And that goes
cluster wide, right?

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:55.630
Or namespace wide, depending
how you roll it out.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:58.090
So the three things that
Istio kind of gives you around

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.470
in those areas of automation
are, one, very uniform

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:02.910
observability, right?

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.830
Metrics logging
tracing right out

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.690
of the box, operational
agility, which

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.340
is a fancy way of
saying I think a lot

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:12.350
of traffic and
networking control,

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:18.737
and then a very deterministic
policy driven security model.

00:06:18.737 --> 00:06:20.320
So if you dig in a
little bit further,

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:23.770
with telemetry, you can
examine all of that traffic

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.110
in and out of those services
and out of your cluster

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.140
as well with very little to no
instrumentation in many cases,

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:30.640
right?

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:31.840
That's incredibly powerful.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.370
You could take
the responsibility

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.862
of adding in things like
latency and tracing outside

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:38.320
of the hands of
developers and say,

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.320
you don't have to worry
about instrumenting this

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.210
as a starting point.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.780
We'll learn a lot just
by putting this thing

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.580
and running it with Istio.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.870
Same thing is true of traffic.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.150
You can take the
networking control outside

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.050
of the application.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:53.337
Make that a loosely
coupled structure.

00:06:53.337 --> 00:06:55.670
So applications can be deployed
however they need to be.

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.470
Your services can be deployed
in whatever way makes sense.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.010
But then the
cluster operator can

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.110
decide how traffic
[INAUDIBLE] should happen,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.730
how rollouts should happen.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.680
And then finally,
security, obviously, right?

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.220
Being able to secure access and
communication between services

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.097
or add authentication policies
to individual services or all

00:07:14.097 --> 00:07:16.180
of them, depending on how
you want to roll it out.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.410
So at a glance, Istio is--

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.240
again, it does add
complexity, right?

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:25.070
Where we're sort of
solving complexity

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.720
by adding some other complexity.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.752
So that's a little bit
of a tradeoff in terms

00:07:28.752 --> 00:07:29.960
of how much it has to happen.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:33.440
But if you think about it, what
you're effectively trying to do

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:36.470
is, one, narrow the
problem space a little bit.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:39.320
You're trying to tell
your application teams,

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.150
take the complexity
away from them.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:44.430
You're saying don't worry
about networking control.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.430
Don't worry about some
of the telemetry aspects.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:48.020
Don't worry about security.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:49.820
We'll take the complexity
away from them,

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.050
and we'll put it on
the cluster operator.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.050
But then we'll give them,
again, that automation

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:57.158
to apply those things in
a much more granular way

00:07:57.158 --> 00:07:59.200
and be able to, again,
incrementally roll it out.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.140
So if you start with the
most basic component,

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.000
that's the Istio sidecar proxy.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.090
And you'll see that
little hexagon over there.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.660
That's actually the Envoy logo.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:13.650
So the Istio sidecar is
powered by Envoy, right?

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.480
It's effectively
the Envoy proxy.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.540
Envoy's a fantastic project,
fantastic piece of code.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.070
It's very high performance.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.530
And because it lives
in your pod right

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.840
alongside your application
container or your services

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.910
container, they share
the same network space,

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:31.050
which means it can actually
capture and filter through all

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.919
the traffic going in and out.

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:35.429
So it adds a thin
little layer of control

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:40.500
over the traffic going in
and out of your containers.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.650
The next part is I guess what
we call sort of the data plane,

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:44.190
right?

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.380
Which things are sort of
generating and pushing data

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:47.730
out or implementing rules?

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.560
Again, that's the Envoy
proxy piece itself,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.080
but when you kind of
slice across your cluster,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.390
we consider those proxies
as the, quote unquote, data

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:55.598
plane in the cluster.

00:08:55.598 --> 00:08:57.390
And this is something
you can deploy today.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.910
You can take the Envoy
open source proxy

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.460
and deploy it as part of your
containers or part of your pods

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:03.720
today.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.080
What's missing is a
central control plane

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:10.387
to deliver rules and
configuration to those proxies.

00:09:10.387 --> 00:09:11.970
And that's really
what Istio provides.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.120
It kind of packages this
whole thing up and gives you

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.730
a unified control plane
with a series of components

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.760
to push rules and
configuration information out.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.260
And so inside of
that control plane,

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:26.275
there's three main
components, right?

00:09:26.275 --> 00:09:27.650
And there's actually
a fourth one

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:28.930
I didn't mention
here, galley, which

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.090
is responsible for handling
some of the configuration

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:31.590
components.

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.520
But the three main
ones that we tend

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.950
to deal with on a regular basis
are pilot, mixer, and citadel.

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:41.692
So pilot's responsibility
is taking in the rules

00:09:41.692 --> 00:09:43.400
that you give it and
making sure they get

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:45.857
back out to all those proxies.

00:09:45.857 --> 00:09:47.815
So at any point in the
lifetime of your cluster

00:09:47.815 --> 00:09:52.840
or your deployment, you can see
all of your pods and the rules

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:55.090
that pilot has sent out and
figure out which ones have

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:57.070
synced and which ones haven't,
which ones have gotten

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:59.350
the latest version of the
rules, which ones haven't.

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:01.480
So you have that control there.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:05.950
Mixer's job is to take metrics
and telemetry data that

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:07.630
come out of those
proxies, and then

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:10.150
send them downstream to
the telemetry backend

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:12.520
of your choice, whether
it's Prometheus,

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:15.070
Grafana, Stackdriver.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.020
You name it.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:19.350
And the last one, citadel,
is responsible for managing

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:20.400
certificates.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.548
So its job is to
make sure everybody's

00:10:22.548 --> 00:10:24.090
got the right
certificates, and keys,

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.660
and authentication information.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:27.690
And it does the magic
of rotating those things

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:28.800
around all on its own.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.690
So that's a little bit of
the components of Istio,

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:37.240
but what does Istio
really do for you, right?

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:38.870
If we get into some
of that detail.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:40.470
So if we start
with observability,

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:43.180
or observing, as
I mentioned, you

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.400
get automatic tracing, logging,
and monitoring by default.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.880
So as long as you send your
logs out to local hosts,

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.700
as long as you publish your
metrics at known good end

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:55.900
points, and whether you
do or you don't actually

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:59.500
add any tracing information,
it can actually still

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:03.920
deliver some tracing information
out from your applications.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:06.130
So those proxies,
they send that tracing

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.020
and that telemetry
data to mixer.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:11.410
And mixer has a
well-formed adapter API

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:14.627
that lets it send all that
data to particular backends.

00:11:14.627 --> 00:11:16.210
And you could, again,
split these out.

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:20.170
So you may say, well, if I'm
running in GKE inside of Google

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:22.600
Cloud, maybe I want to
use Stackdriver logging

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:23.770
in Stackdriver trace.

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.630
But you really love
Prometheus and Grafana.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.430
By all means, you can
put those two together.

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.347
So you can have that mix
of different backends

00:11:29.347 --> 00:11:32.380
and components depending
on the piece you want.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:35.290
You could even have
it so you push metrics

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:37.850
to Prometheus and to
Stackdriver if you wanted to.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.360
There's no real limit to
how you can configure this.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:45.490
For connecting services,
right, that kind

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:47.590
of operational agility
component is really

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:49.550
where the networking
piece comes in.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:52.150
There's a few objects that are
really dedicated specifically

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:53.535
to networking inside of Istio.

00:11:53.535 --> 00:11:57.880
And those are virtual services,
destination rules, gateways,

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:59.260
and service entries.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.990
And so those, in some
combination of one or more

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:05.500
of those, they let you do things
like traffic splitting, traffic

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:08.650
steering, right, from one
version to another, fault

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:13.220
injection, circuit braking,
and egress control,

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:14.730
which is really powerful, right?

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:18.280
If you have a lot of services
that call external APIs,

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:21.010
this is a really powerful way
to put some control over that,

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.710
but more importantly,
as a security mechanism

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.080
or to form a better
security posture.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.142
Let's say you have a bad
docker image that you pulled

00:12:29.142 --> 00:12:31.600
and deployed, maybe a malformed
tag, or maybe something got

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:33.290
injected into that container.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:35.470
Well, if you lock
down egress control,

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:38.770
that means nobody
can make outbound API

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:41.260
calls or outbound calls,
unless you've specifically

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.097
allowed that service.

00:12:43.097 --> 00:12:45.680
So now you're getting just sort
of this free kind of perimeter

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.140
around your deployment,
just by having that proxy

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:51.890
and having this
infrastructure in place.

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:54.650
If we dig in a little bit to
some of the traffic splitting

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:59.240
and traffic steering, the
way the Envoy proxy works

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.550
is you send rules up to
pilot in that configuration.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:05.460
Pilot sends those rules to
end those individual proxies.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.970
So now those proxies themselves
know how to route traffic

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:09.930
around from one to the other.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:12.590
So in the top example, we've
got service A and service

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:18.260
B. Service B has different
versions that that might have.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.082
If you just rely on
Kubernetes routing,

00:13:21.082 --> 00:13:22.540
you get round robin
routing, right?

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:24.348
So you basically get
an equivalent amount

00:13:24.348 --> 00:13:25.640
of requests spread across here.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:28.430
So in the example, in the
right side and the upper right

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:33.320
there example, you might see
like 25% going to each pod.

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:35.810
With Istio, we can
actually control this

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:37.610
and say, well, we want 95 and 5.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:41.890
We want to have a canary version
that we roll out very slowly.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:43.390
On the bottom
example, that's really

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:45.730
where we get into things
like traffic steering.

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:47.440
Again, because of
the way Envoy works,

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:49.480
it can actually
inspect the headers

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.603
coming in to your applications.

00:13:51.603 --> 00:13:53.020
So that means that
we can actually

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:55.270
say based on maybe
a header value,

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:57.880
I might want to direct traffic
to a particular version

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.182
or particular pod.

00:13:59.182 --> 00:14:00.640
So for example,
you could say, hey,

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:06.580
for logged in users running on
iOS with WebKit version xyz,

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:08.310
direct them to this
canary rollout test

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:10.640
or this other version
of that service.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:12.700
So you have that
level of control

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.230
that you didn't
have just, again,

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:16.410
with sort of vanilla Kubernetes.

00:14:18.973 --> 00:14:20.640
And as I mentioned
earlier, you can also

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:22.868
inject things along the way.

00:14:22.868 --> 00:14:24.660
And these are really
not necessarily things

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:26.660
you're going to use in a
production environment,

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:29.560
but for more of a testing
and resiliency perspective.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:33.120
You may want to inject things
like delay, sort of retries,

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.910
or even other connection rules.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.390
Circuit breaker
is a good example.

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:39.360
You can inject a
circuit breaker inside

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:43.800
of this path between services,
and control whether you want

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:45.000
cascade failures to happen.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:46.860
Or if requests
start to fail, just

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:48.360
cut off access immediately.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.617
So your end users aren't seeing
high latency waiting for errors

00:14:51.617 --> 00:14:52.950
to time out all the way through.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:57.850
And as with a lot of
the way Istio works,

00:14:57.850 --> 00:15:00.970
those rules can match
very particular conditions

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:03.550
and be directed at a
percentage of requests,

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:05.350
not all requests or just some.

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:07.570
So you have, again,
very granular control

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:11.990
that you can really implement
across your platform.

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:14.210
On the security front,
four main components that

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:15.710
really stand out, right?

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:18.695
Encrypting traffic
between services,

00:15:18.695 --> 00:15:20.820
authenticating services or
service authentication--

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.090
service to service,
I should say--

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:26.900
auditing control, and access
control policies, right?

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:28.400
Again, very powerful stuff.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:29.480
Think of it this way.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.470
I can implement--
again, mutual TLS

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:32.990
is a good example
and actually one

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:34.670
that a customer came to us with.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:37.070
They were told by one of
their downstream customers

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.740
that they needed to encrypt
traffic between all services.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:42.110
And Istio was the fastest
way for them to get there.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:45.260
Because they redeployed their
pods with the sidecar proxy

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:47.030
and then flipped a
bit with one rule

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:50.715
to turn on mutual authentication
across everything there.

00:15:50.715 --> 00:15:51.840
And that's really powerful.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.072
The security model--
this diagram's

00:15:57.072 --> 00:15:58.780
a little bit hard to
read in a few spots.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:00.530
The point is it's kind
of rolling together

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.740
all of the security parts
of the infrastructure

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:04.840
that you can kind of
inject along the way.

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:07.008
So requests are coming
in on the far left

00:16:07.008 --> 00:16:09.550
and sort of traversing through
to the far right on the egress

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:10.490
side.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:13.780
And you can see where citadels
issuing certificates, where

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:15.880
things like service
authentication maybe

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:20.220
with job tokens or MTLS
between services is happening.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.180
So you have, again,
this level of control

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.510
that's not really possible
without another higher level

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:25.010
tool.

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:32.730
So that's a little bit of
the background of the why

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:34.510
and sort of the what of Istio.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:37.070
But how do you put Istio to
work for yourself, right?

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:39.770
This is where I want to get
to this idea of incrementally

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:40.700
rolling it out.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:43.022
Because like with
any new technology,

00:16:43.022 --> 00:16:44.870
you don't honestly
want to start and go

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.090
from zero to 100% on day one.

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:50.270
You want to find the features
that make the most sense,

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:54.770
and then start rolling
those out bit by bit.

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:58.570
So the easiest way to get
started with Istio is actually,

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:01.000
we built something inside
of Google Kubernetes

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:02.830
Engine, a little
add-on that lets

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:05.800
you deploy Istio
by default or along

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:07.420
with your cluster deployment.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:10.609
So using either G Cloud
or the Cloud console,

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:12.526
you can actually spin
up a Kubernetes cluster.

00:17:12.526 --> 00:17:14.734
And there's a little section
called add-ons, and just

00:17:14.734 --> 00:17:15.880
check the box for Istio.

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.800
And then pick a security
approach, whether you

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:19.030
want permissive or strict.

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:21.640
And for the purposes
of this example,

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:23.680
I used MTLS permissive,
just to make

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:24.805
things a little bit easier.

00:17:24.805 --> 00:17:26.513
But that's something
I started with here.

00:17:26.513 --> 00:17:28.119
So this is an easy
way to get Istio.

00:17:28.119 --> 00:17:30.250
But frankly, installing
the OSS version

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:32.320
is not that terribly
challenging either, right?

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:33.850
Grab the latest release.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:36.708
Apply it via the Helm chart,
and you're off and running.

00:17:36.708 --> 00:17:39.250
And with the Helm chart, you
get the level of customizability

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:39.875
you might want.

00:17:39.875 --> 00:17:42.820
So for example, maybe
you decide today, I only

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:44.920
really care about telemetry.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.080
Only get the components
that worry about telemetry.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.120
Don't worry about having
the traffic components

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:50.500
or the security components.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:53.580
You can always upgrade to
that later on using Helm.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.833
So Helm gives you that
power of flexibility.

00:17:55.833 --> 00:17:58.000
And there's also a quick
start inside the Istio repo

00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.952
as well that just
deploys everything.

00:17:59.952 --> 00:18:01.660
So you have a couple
of different options

00:18:01.660 --> 00:18:05.980
for how to get the software
running inside of your cluster.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:08.270
Once you do this and once
this cluster comes up,

00:18:08.270 --> 00:18:11.560
you end up with a bunch of pods
running inside the Istio system

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:12.082
namespace.

00:18:12.082 --> 00:18:13.540
And again, it's
all those things we

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:18.300
saw earlier-- pilot, citadel,
galley, among other things

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.657
depending on which
version you installed, OSS

00:18:20.657 --> 00:18:22.990
or whether you're using the
one we package here for you.

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:25.440
If you use this version, we
install all the Stackdriver

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:28.310
adapters for you by
default. If you use OSS,

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.680
it comes with Prometheus
and Grafana out of the box.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.972
So you have a couple of
different options there.

00:18:32.972 --> 00:18:35.430
But once you've got this up
and running, once the system is

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:40.070
ready, you can actually start
to include the sidecar proxy

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:41.360
inside of your pods.

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.190
And there's two ways to do it.

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:45.230
The bottom way is the
way we see a lot of folks

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:48.110
do it, which is basically
taking a namespace and saying,

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:50.700
anything that gets deployed
to that namespace, by default,

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:53.412
just auto inject
to sidecar proxy.

00:18:53.412 --> 00:18:55.370
Or you have the other
approach of saying, well,

00:18:55.370 --> 00:18:57.890
I'm going to do it on a
per sort of YAML basis.

00:18:57.890 --> 00:18:59.510
Every time I push
a new service, I'm

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:03.350
going to filter my YAML
through this Istio control

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:07.010
binary that adds in
the appropriate piece

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:08.630
to your pod spec.

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:11.598
So it includes a sidecar proxy.

00:19:11.598 --> 00:19:13.640
So there's a couple
different ways to kind of get

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:14.223
started there.

00:19:16.313 --> 00:19:18.230
But if you're rolling
it out, what I would say

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:20.190
is don't start with
auto injection, right?

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:22.110
You don't want the
pod out right yet.

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:23.958
Again, it really comes
down to the features

00:19:23.958 --> 00:19:25.250
that you're most interested in.

00:19:27.823 --> 00:19:29.240
The other thing I
would also say--

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:30.740
and I mentioned
this a bit earlier--

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:32.070
is start with permissive MTLS.

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:33.540
You can always turn it on later.

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:35.810
But when you first turn it
on, if you go with strict,

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:36.970
it might start to break things.

00:19:36.970 --> 00:19:38.345
So you might want
to roll it out,

00:19:38.345 --> 00:19:40.970
again, on a case by case or
service by service basis.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.450
So that's just a little
bit of getting started.

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:49.200
Let's go through a couple
of or a few example kind

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.600
of incremental rollouts, right?

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:53.520
Then again, think of
the state of the cluster

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.320
as we've got Istio
up and running.

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:59.930
We're not auto injecting
the sidecar proxy yet.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:03.242
So for traffic management,
let's say you've

00:20:03.242 --> 00:20:04.450
got a simple deployment here.

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:06.460
And I wrote a little sample
app called Weather Info

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:08.960
because I was getting really
tired of the built-in Bookinfo.

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:10.690
I just decided to
make another one.

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:13.390
And it's got a frontend and
two different backends, right?

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:16.630
This just pulls the weather
from an external API.

00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:18.580
v1 pulls it from a single city.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:21.010
And v2 pulls it for four cities
and just displays it out,

00:20:21.010 --> 00:20:22.300
so nothing fancy.

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:24.640
But let's say we want to
roll this out in a way,

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:26.902
again, using Istio
incrementally,

00:20:26.902 --> 00:20:28.360
but we still want
to take advantage

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.675
of some of the traffic
features that are available.

00:20:30.675 --> 00:20:31.550
So how do we do that?

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:34.960
Right?

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:37.420
Quick recap on traffic
splitting just to remember,

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:38.480
just to remind y'all.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:38.980
Right?

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.438
We will have this
approach where we

00:20:40.438 --> 00:20:41.710
want to try to get this 95/5.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:44.890
Now the difference here is
that all of these examples,

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:46.980
they've got the
sidecar proxy in them.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:49.480
So the question is-- and this
is the question I asked myself

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:50.530
about six months ago--

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:52.990
how can I do this kind
of traffic splitting

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.865
without having to roll
the sidecar proxy out?

00:20:54.865 --> 00:20:57.115
It turns out there's a little
bit of a cheat and a way

00:20:57.115 --> 00:20:57.800
to do it.

00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:01.460
It's not initially the way I
would recommend for everything,

00:21:01.460 --> 00:21:04.090
especially for full blown
kind of large scale production

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:04.720
deployments.

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:06.790
But as you're testing
things out and getting

00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:08.840
acquainted with the
rules and infrastructure,

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:11.530
it's a way to kind of play
with the networking APIs

00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:15.460
before, again, you
go wide with it.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.100
So what we're going
to try to do here,

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:18.790
our goal is to get to 90/10.

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:21.390
And remember, without Istio,
right, without anything

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:24.270
by default, Kubernetes will
just do round robin 50/50

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:26.560
across both of these.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.060
So that's where
we're starting from.

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:29.410
We're starting from 50/50.

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:32.218
Now this little box
there is Istio, right?

00:21:32.218 --> 00:21:33.760
And I've got a little
ingress gateway

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:37.550
that's allowing inbound
traffic at the top.

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:39.370
So for us to get
to this, there's

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:42.310
a few components we have
to deploy to make it work.

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:45.700
So using our virtual service
and a destination rule,

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:48.130
we can actually direct
traffic from weather frontend

00:21:48.130 --> 00:21:51.940
to weather backend v1
at 90% and v2 at 10%

00:21:51.940 --> 00:21:55.580
by directing traffic
through the ingress gateway.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:57.540
So again, rolling it
out a little bit slowly,

00:21:57.540 --> 00:21:59.130
but getting a chance
to test this out

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:00.465
in kind of a measured approach.

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:05.150
So the traffic
policy we want to do

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:06.915
for this is the
first thing we do

00:22:06.915 --> 00:22:08.220
is create a destination rule.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:10.170
And as you can
imagine by the name,

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:13.380
a destination rule kind of tells
inbound traffic where to go.

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.510
So it's saying I want
to direct traffic

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.040
to these possible subsets.

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.230
So in this example, all
the destination rule

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:22.530
is really saying is create
some subsets of my service.

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:24.380
In this case,
single and multiple

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:25.770
is what I call them, right?

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.392
So we've got a subset for single
and a subset for multiple.

00:22:28.392 --> 00:22:29.850
I just want to have
those on record

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:32.250
as being things I can
direct traffic to later on.

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:37.090
And then we create
the gateway component.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:40.893
We connect the gateway to the
rest of the infrastructure.

00:22:40.893 --> 00:22:42.310
So what we're
saying here is we're

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:44.982
going to open up a
port 80 on the gateway.

00:22:44.982 --> 00:22:46.440
We're going to do
it for all hosts.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:47.898
Again, this is just
a demo example.

00:22:47.898 --> 00:22:49.350
I wouldn't do this
in real world.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:50.430
I wouldn't do this
in production.

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:51.888
All we're telling
you here is we're

00:22:51.888 --> 00:22:54.960
going to set up a gateway
connected to the Istio ingress

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:57.330
gateway that comes with Istio
by default, which is just

00:22:57.330 --> 00:22:58.780
another Envoy proxy.

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:02.958
And we're going to tell
it to open up port 80.

00:23:02.958 --> 00:23:05.000
And then we can actually
create a virtual service

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:08.420
that tells requests when they
get to the ingress gateway

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:09.860
where to direct them to.

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:11.690
So remember, we had
those named subsets

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:14.100
of single and multiple
a couple of slides back.

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:17.150
So here, we can call
it out specifically.

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:19.910
We attach this virtual
service to that gateway

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:21.440
we just created.

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:23.990
We match on a very particular
endpoint-- in this case,

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:26.012
/api/weather.

00:23:26.012 --> 00:23:27.470
And then we tell
it to just traffic

00:23:27.470 --> 00:23:29.990
split those two across
the single version

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:34.028
and the multiple version
at a 90/10 split.

00:23:34.028 --> 00:23:34.570
So that's it.

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:35.070
We're done.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:37.540
Any traffic coming in
through that ingress gateway

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:39.920
is now going to go
across those two subsets.

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:40.545
So that's good.

00:23:40.545 --> 00:23:42.920
We've solved some of the basic
traffic splitting problem,

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:45.370
but we haven't actually
connected the weather frontend

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:47.400
component to it yet.

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:53.860
So the last step in that process
is to update the deployment.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:56.560
All we're changing here is we're
changing the backend location.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:58.810
So this is actually in the
weather frontend deployment

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:02.720
spec for the component's
frontend Kubernetes.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:04.720
All it did was change the
hostname of where it's

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:06.160
supposed to send traffic to.

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:08.285
Now it's going to send it
through the Istio ingress

00:24:08.285 --> 00:24:09.580
gateway instead.

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:11.880
So really, the only service
that had to change here,

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:13.790
that had to be
redeployed, if you will,

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:15.697
was the weather
frontend component.

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:20.063
Once you push this
out, all the traffic

00:24:20.063 --> 00:24:21.980
will now start to go
through that other piece,

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:23.150
the ingress gateway.

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:25.070
And then it will
get split there.

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:27.710
So we did this without
deploying any Envoy proxies

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:30.470
across those pods
that were there.

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:31.940
Again, the nice
way to do this is--

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:34.130
or the nice thing about
this is that you're not

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:36.025
having to redeploy or
update infrastructure.

00:24:36.025 --> 00:24:38.672
You're not having to
inject a sidecar quite yet.

00:24:38.672 --> 00:24:40.130
And it's a good
way to sort of test

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:42.740
and get familiarity
with the components,

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:45.380
with the APIs that
are available to you.

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:47.630
But that's one example of
just kind of rolling it out.

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:51.590
What about security?

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:54.260
How do you roll out security
in a measured and controlled

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:56.880
manner?

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.702
So in this case, we
are going to have

00:24:58.702 --> 00:25:00.410
to have injection
turnout because really,

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:03.110
at the end of the day, for you
to make use of any security

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:06.110
infrastructure, you've got to
have the sidecar proxy included

00:25:06.110 --> 00:25:09.030
in those pods to
enforce those policies.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:10.850
So here, what we
did is we've got

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:12.860
two namespaces in
our cluster, one

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:16.520
I called legacy, which
is some vanilla service,

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:18.807
and one called secure.

00:25:18.807 --> 00:25:20.515
And in the secure
namespace, I have Istio

00:25:20.515 --> 00:25:22.010
and auto injection turned on.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.968
So that means when I
deployed weather frontend,

00:25:23.968 --> 00:25:27.950
weather backend, they got the
Envoy proxy in those pods.

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:31.100
So now what we want to do is we
want to slowly roll out MTLS.

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:33.350
We want to roll it out so
we secure the infrastructure

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:34.800
in the secure namespace.

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:38.510
And we want to allow traffic
from the legacy namespace.

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:44.160
So the first thing we do
after we get these services up

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:47.100
and running is start
with a security policy.

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:49.953
And for those of you that are
paying really close attention,

00:25:49.953 --> 00:25:51.870
I had to shorten the
names of the API versions

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:54.180
to fit on the slide.

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:56.310
So if you happen to
look at these later on,

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:57.300
don't copy from here.

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:59.160
Copy from the GitHub repo.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:02.690
That actually has the
correct YAML specified there.

00:26:02.690 --> 00:26:07.240
But here, what we did is we're
setting up the backend service.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:10.100
We're turning on a policy
there for security.

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:12.650
We're saying we want it to
have a permissive policy so

00:26:12.650 --> 00:26:16.420
that anybody can connect to
it from within that cluster.

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:18.210
So the net effect is
that this service now

00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:20.020
has access to connect here.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:20.760
Right?

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:22.860
So now it has a permissive
policy you can connect.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:24.600
And weather frontend was
not impacted by this.

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:26.660
It was because it's got
the Envoy proxy already.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:27.868
It can already connect to it.

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:32.440
And now what we can do is
apply a destination rule

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:36.830
that's saying anybody that's got
the proxy as part of their pod

00:26:36.830 --> 00:26:39.260
can use mutual authentication.

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:41.980
So what this is doing is,
again, slowly rolling out

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:46.310
MTLS to all the services that
can take advantage of it.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:47.890
So this destination
rule, all it says

00:26:47.890 --> 00:26:51.140
is use the Istio mutual mode.

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:53.920
That means if you are
capable of doing MTLS,

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:56.142
let's go ahead and do it.

00:26:56.142 --> 00:26:57.850
So the net effect
there is that now we've

00:26:57.850 --> 00:27:00.740
got mutual TLS happening between
frontend and the backend,

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:04.990
but still maintaining permissive
access to weather backend.

00:27:04.990 --> 00:27:08.003
By the way, and that rule was
kind of under specified, right?

00:27:08.003 --> 00:27:10.420
I left it a little bit open
about who could connect to it.

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:12.250
You can actually get
way more fine grained

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:14.470
and say only
certain services can

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:16.270
utilize that permissive mode.

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:17.927
So you have that
ability to control

00:27:17.927 --> 00:27:19.510
at a very fine grained
level about who

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:24.600
can access that thing without
using mutual authentication.

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:27.170
But now what were to happen
if we turn on strict, right?

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:33.670
If we take it from a permissive
mode or mutual mode to strict,

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:34.210
right?

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:36.377
Once we implement this
policy and update that policy

00:27:36.377 --> 00:27:38.980
from saying permissive to
strict, that means anybody that

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:42.610
doesn't have the proxy
that can't do MTLS

00:27:42.610 --> 00:27:45.308
won't be able to
connect to this.

00:27:45.308 --> 00:27:47.350
Because what they're
missing are the certificates

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:49.930
from things like citadel and
the configuration from galley

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:51.640
to make it work.

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:54.580
So this is an easy way to
start with-- right, again,

00:27:54.580 --> 00:27:57.460
remember the cluster we deployed
was set to permissive mode.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:59.990
So that was already
kind of an open setup.

00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:04.480
But with mutual, we actually
able to turn on MTLS again

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:06.220
on that service
by service basis.

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:08.890
And anybody that could
do it would use it.

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:10.630
And then once we
transitioned to strict,

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:13.175
we cut off access
to legacy clients.

00:28:13.175 --> 00:28:15.550
So this is a good way to think
about-- again, it's a demo

00:28:15.550 --> 00:28:17.140
and I couldn't fit that
much information on a slide,

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:19.450
but think about how you
might, again, slowly

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:22.150
roll this kind of thing
out across a larger

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:23.470
environment, right?

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:28.270
If you map this kind of problem
to 5, 500, 1,000 services,

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:31.420
it's an easy way to enforce
policy and enforce security

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:33.970
very quickly and to
start encrypting traffic

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:35.990
between services immediately.

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:41.590
So that was an incremental
rollout of security.

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:43.080
What about telemetry, right?

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:45.000
This is another, I
think, key feature

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:47.100
of Istio, where, again,
you get that monitoring

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:48.780
and that tracing and
logging for free.

00:28:51.410 --> 00:28:53.160
And I mentioned this
a little bit earlier,

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:56.190
but I love hitting
it as much as I can.

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:58.230
Without additional
instrumentation,

00:28:58.230 --> 00:29:01.200
you get this output directed
to your choice of backend.

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:06.180
So what are some of the metrics
that you get by default,

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:07.800
that you get for free?

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:11.150
So you get things like request
count, request duration.

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:12.900
Particularly for
HTTP, you get things

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:16.045
like size of request body,
the response body size.

00:29:16.045 --> 00:29:18.420
And then for TCP services,
you get additional information

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:21.110
like bytes sent, bytes received.

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:23.610
And this is actually a first
source and destination as well,

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:25.390
so you get variations for both.

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:26.970
So you can actually
track, again,

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:30.240
traffic inbound to your
pod, traffic outbound.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:32.310
And control and
understand the telemetry

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:33.453
and what's happening there.

00:29:33.453 --> 00:29:35.370
So these metrics just
start getting published.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:38.727
And again, in open source Istio,
they go straight to Prometheus.

00:29:38.727 --> 00:29:40.560
In the version that you
can install directly

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:44.020
through the GKE console, they
go straight to Stackdriver.

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:44.520
That's it.

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:49.610
So at this point, what I'd like
to do is switch to the demo.

00:29:56.625 --> 00:29:57.750
Can everybody read that OK?

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:59.800
Or is that big enough?

00:29:59.800 --> 00:30:00.900
Right, no one's a no.

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:01.545
So let's see.

00:30:01.545 --> 00:30:03.920
We're going to make sure we're
on the right cluster here.

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:06.808
So I'm in my hybrid 102 cluster.

00:30:06.808 --> 00:30:08.600
And we can show you
what's running on here.

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:13.133
So I've got that
example app I talked

00:30:13.133 --> 00:30:14.550
about before, the
weather frontend

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:17.472
with the weather backend single,
weather backend multiple.

00:30:17.472 --> 00:30:18.930
And I've got a
little low generator

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:22.040
script that's generating
and pushing traffic through.

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:23.700
And so what I wanted
to show you first

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:28.180
is the Stackdriver
Trace component.

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:30.577
So again, I didn't change
anything in my code.

00:30:30.577 --> 00:30:31.410
I didn't instrument.

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:32.850
I didn't add anything to it.

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:36.990
All I did is turned on Istio,
deployed those containers

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:39.060
with the sidecar
proxy auto injected,

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:41.340
and then I started sending
data to Stackdriver Trace

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:43.120
immediately.

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:44.730
And so I just did
this earlier today

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:47.150
so that doesn't have an
automatic report there,

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:49.510
but we can actually dig
in a little bit further.

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:51.435
So this is a
Stackdriver trace UI.

00:30:51.435 --> 00:30:52.920
It will by default
give you things

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:54.900
like insights over
the last seven days,

00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:57.750
or it'll create some auto
analysis reports for you.

00:30:57.750 --> 00:31:00.030
Or you can actually just
dig into individual URLs

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:02.880
or requests yourself.

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:05.890
So if I go over here
to the trace list,

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:08.050
I can actually look and see--

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:10.592
I can look at the latency of
the requests that are coming in.

00:31:10.592 --> 00:31:12.633
Again, everything is being
generated by that load

00:31:12.633 --> 00:31:13.780
generator script right now.

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:16.340
But actually, drag along
here and figure out.

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:18.520
So let's pick this
little section there.

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:20.277
I had a couple of high requests.

00:31:20.277 --> 00:31:22.360
So I can look here and say
I've got a request that

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:25.200
was 301 milliseconds.

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:27.900
If I dig into that
one completely, one,

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:30.930
I get this timeline, so I
can see again the request

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:32.093
and how long it took.

00:31:32.093 --> 00:31:33.510
And then over here
on the right, I

00:31:33.510 --> 00:31:37.240
get a lot more detail about
things like the timestamp.

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:39.640
And then I get to see things
like where it came from.

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:41.740
And I think that's where
it's really important.

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:43.990
I can actually go here and
say the source workload was

00:31:43.990 --> 00:31:45.510
weather frontend, right?

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:47.820
The URL was API weather.

00:31:47.820 --> 00:31:49.440
This agent that's
using the track

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:51.660
is the agent that's built
into the sidecar proxy

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:53.270
or built into
mixer, I should say.

00:31:53.270 --> 00:31:54.645
And where that
traffic was going.

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:00.890
So we get some level
of detail here.

00:32:00.890 --> 00:32:03.290
I can also filter and go back.

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:06.410
And let's say I want to just
look at the external API

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:10.430
calls I'm making to
the weather map API.

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:14.320
So I can say Sent.api and
filter requests there.

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:16.760
And now here I can see
that most of my requests

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:19.910
are staying in this under
50 milliseconds boundary.

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:22.420
But we've got a couple that
have taken a little bit longer.

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:24.470
And we can see,
again, the timeline

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:27.900
it took for that
request to go out

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:30.060
and the workload that
that came from, right?

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:31.920
So in this case, it
came from the weather

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:33.880
backend multiple service.

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:38.563
And here's the actual
URL it was requesting.

00:32:38.563 --> 00:32:39.980
And I didn't add
any tracing here.

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:43.250
So I haven't done anything
to my code to enable this.

00:32:43.250 --> 00:32:46.040
Now as you can
probably imagine, this

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:49.200
isn't telling me a whole heck
of a lot of information, right?

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:52.520
Because Envoy can
track those requests

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:55.790
from one proxy to another,
it's capable of showing

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:56.940
you this data.

00:32:56.940 --> 00:32:59.330
If you want to get that
next level of information,

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:02.120
if you want to know, well,
once it enters that request,

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:05.918
once that request hits one
of my methods in my code,

00:33:05.918 --> 00:33:08.210
I want to know which line or
which section of that code

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:09.415
is taking a long time.

00:33:09.415 --> 00:33:10.790
If you want that
level of detail,

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:12.717
you'll have to actually
go in and instrument.

00:33:12.717 --> 00:33:15.050
And you'll have your choice
of different instrumentation

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:18.020
libraries, like either
Zipkin or OpenCensus,

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:20.280
depending on which one makes
the most sense for you.

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:22.700
But once you have that
level of instrumentation,

00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:26.480
what you would actually see here
is a much more granular kind

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:29.060
of bar graph of the different
sections of your code

00:33:29.060 --> 00:33:30.380
or how long they took.

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:32.330
So you get a much
better and clearer idea

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:35.101
of what's actually
happening across there.

00:33:35.101 --> 00:33:36.900
The nice thing about
that is you don't only

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:39.485
have to add the tracing
statements into your code.

00:33:39.485 --> 00:33:41.610
You don't actually to worry
about where to send it.

00:33:41.610 --> 00:33:44.910
Because again, the proxy
is capturing that tracing

00:33:44.910 --> 00:33:47.225
reporting data on its way out.

00:33:47.225 --> 00:33:49.100
And it's actually
forwarding it on the mixer,

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:51.267
and the mixer is actually
posting it to the backend.

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:57.010
So that's a quick run through
of the tracing components

00:33:57.010 --> 00:34:00.070
here, instead of
Stackdriver Trace.

00:34:00.070 --> 00:34:01.780
Let me quickly show
you all what it

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:04.060
looks like if you were to
look at the open source

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:06.370
Prometheus and Grafana.

00:34:06.370 --> 00:34:11.989
So let me just quickly forward
the port over from Prometheus.

00:34:11.989 --> 00:34:14.120
And I'm doing this from a
different cluster that's

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:15.245
running in another project.

00:34:25.850 --> 00:34:28.199
All right, so that's
forwarding across.

00:34:28.199 --> 00:34:30.620
We can open up Prometheus now.

00:34:30.620 --> 00:34:32.639
And inside Prometheus,
if you've ever

00:34:32.639 --> 00:34:34.770
seen the sort of default
Prometheus piece here,

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:37.840
you can actually start
filtering by Istio metrics.

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:39.840
So you can look at sort
of Istio total requests.

00:34:44.753 --> 00:34:46.170
Which is not very
exciting, right,

00:34:46.170 --> 00:34:47.257
looking through all this.

00:34:47.257 --> 00:34:49.090
It's a lot of information
and a lot of data.

00:34:49.090 --> 00:34:52.290
It's not really sort of
eminently visible by default

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:54.719
here, even if you
kind of graph it out.

00:34:54.719 --> 00:34:57.280
What's nicer is when you
dig into it using Grafana.

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:00.450
So the Prometheus and Grafana
that come with OSS Istio

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:03.960
are pre-configured to be tightly
coupled and worked together.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:06.900
So Grafana is already pulling
data right off that Prometheus

00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:07.930
instance.

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:11.080
So if I do the same thing here,
but change that port forwarding

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:16.670
to Grafana, you can actually
see a lot of interesting metrics

00:35:16.670 --> 00:35:17.510
in Grafana as well.

00:35:20.860 --> 00:35:23.370
The other nice thing about
the instance of Grafana

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:26.960
that we package in for you is
that it comes with some canned

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:29.615
Istio specific dashboards.

00:35:29.615 --> 00:35:31.740
So once you load the Grafana
on the main dashboard,

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:34.200
you go over to kind of
the manage section, which

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:35.020
is what I did.

00:35:35.020 --> 00:35:36.818
And there should be
an Istio folder there.

00:35:36.818 --> 00:35:38.110
You can open that Istio folder.

00:35:38.110 --> 00:35:39.160
And now we can
actually start to say,

00:35:39.160 --> 00:35:41.430
well, I want to dig into
the service dashboard

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:43.550
in particular.

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:45.950
Inside the service
dashboard, right, it

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:48.140
opens with any of the
services that I'm calling.

00:35:48.140 --> 00:35:51.110
So because I have a service
entry inside of Istio

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:54.620
for api.openweathermap.org,
it creates

00:35:54.620 --> 00:35:57.170
a service entry for that
here so I can browse by that.

00:35:57.170 --> 00:36:00.050
So I can actually see the client
request volume, the success

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:00.720
rate.

00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:02.250
This one's not that interesting.

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:03.875
But if we look at
the weather backend,

00:36:03.875 --> 00:36:06.200
that gets little bit
more interesting, right?

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:10.740
We could see the request
volume, the success rate.

00:36:10.740 --> 00:36:14.170
And if we continue on down,
incoming requests and response

00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:15.970
code, right?

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:18.850
The success rate or the
duration for some of these

00:36:18.850 --> 00:36:20.530
calls, again,
highlighting our ability

00:36:20.530 --> 00:36:23.050
to dig into these calls
that might be taking

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:24.280
quite a long time, right?

00:36:24.280 --> 00:36:26.515
This one is showing us
that that 99th percentile,

00:36:26.515 --> 00:36:29.660
it's returning about
950 milliseconds,

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:31.755
which is quite a bit.

00:36:31.755 --> 00:36:33.380
And there's some TCP
specific ones here

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:34.760
that aren't filled in as well.

00:36:37.630 --> 00:36:39.810
Same thing is true again
of the weather frontend.

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:42.090
You can see those
dashboards and look

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:45.800
at that traffic information.

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:48.370
Or you can switch to the--

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:52.040
let's see, where is the
Istio workload dashboard?

00:36:52.040 --> 00:36:52.540
Right?

00:36:52.540 --> 00:36:54.707
Another way to kind of look
at the information here.

00:36:54.707 --> 00:36:57.100
So we'll switch to
the default namespace.

00:36:57.100 --> 00:36:59.470
And we'll look at
weather backend multiple.

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:02.990
Let's pick on just
one of those services.

00:37:02.990 --> 00:37:06.660
So again, we can see how
long things are taking,

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:09.840
the duration by source,
response size, et cetera.

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:13.670
And we can see all the outbound
and outgoing requests as well.

00:37:13.670 --> 00:37:14.950
So this is built in, right?

00:37:14.950 --> 00:37:16.910
All those metrics I
mentioned earlier,

00:37:16.910 --> 00:37:18.858
those things automatically
captured by Envoy,

00:37:18.858 --> 00:37:21.400
Envoy collects that telemetry
and then sends it off to Mixer.

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:23.580
Mixer forwards it on from you.

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:26.380
And if you remember the
Istio at a glance image,

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:28.450
right, there's a lot of
control plane components.

00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:30.070
And they function
very much the same

00:37:30.070 --> 00:37:32.500
as the Kubernetes
control plane does.

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:35.440
If one of those pieces goes
down for some unspecified amount

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:40.720
of time, you're not locked out
of the traffic routing, right?

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:43.510
You might not be able to push
any new rules or new policies

00:37:43.510 --> 00:37:46.703
while maybe pilot is down, but
the rules that were already

00:37:46.703 --> 00:37:48.370
pushed out and in
place, those are still

00:37:48.370 --> 00:37:50.440
being honored by Envoy.

00:37:50.440 --> 00:37:52.330
Just like if you lose
the Kubernetes API

00:37:52.330 --> 00:37:54.220
master for a certain
amount of time,

00:37:54.220 --> 00:37:56.140
your pods and your
containers are OK.

00:37:56.140 --> 00:37:57.860
You just can't
schedule anything new.

00:37:57.860 --> 00:37:59.777
So you've got a little
bit of a window of time

00:37:59.777 --> 00:38:03.350
before things really turn
ugly to fix things up.

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:06.880
And particularly in the case of
telemetry, if Mixer goes down

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:09.060
or if you lose access
to that Mixer component,

00:38:09.060 --> 00:38:11.410
Envoy will actually
retry to send telemetry.

00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:14.590
So you won't have these
dropouts over time of metrics

00:38:14.590 --> 00:38:17.380
missing because Mixer wasn't
available for some bit there.

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:22.130
So can we switch
back to the slides?

00:38:25.455 --> 00:38:26.283
Perfect.

00:38:26.283 --> 00:38:27.950
All right, so where
do you go from here?

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:28.360
Right?

00:38:28.360 --> 00:38:30.568
We have a couple of examples
or a few examples of how

00:38:30.568 --> 00:38:33.820
you can roll Istio out
in a slow measured manner

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:35.740
across an existing
infrastructure, existing

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:36.820
cluster.

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:39.790
And I would say that the
two features that stand out

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:42.730
among what we hear from
customers and users

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:47.260
in the ecosystem are
telemetry and security, right?

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:49.840
Folks really love the ability
to sort of deploy Istio

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:52.720
and then get this telemetry
information very quickly.

00:38:52.720 --> 00:38:56.200
Because it's quite a bit to be
able to use right off the bat.

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:58.120
And they also love the
security aspect, right?

00:38:58.120 --> 00:38:59.900
Those two features
alone, I think,

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:02.692
are enough to make
Istio worth it.

00:39:02.692 --> 00:39:04.150
So what I would
challenge you to do

00:39:04.150 --> 00:39:05.860
is think about if Istio
makes sense for you

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:08.277
in a production workload,
figure out which of those things

00:39:08.277 --> 00:39:08.960
makes sense.

00:39:08.960 --> 00:39:11.740
Do you want it for traffic,
telemetry, security?

00:39:11.740 --> 00:39:13.360
And then spend
enough time where you

00:39:13.360 --> 00:39:15.790
feel comfortable with
those APIs before you

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:16.780
start rolling it out.

00:39:16.780 --> 00:39:18.905
And do it again in that
incremental fashion, right?

00:39:18.905 --> 00:39:21.113
You can actually roll things
out and say, well, we're

00:39:21.113 --> 00:39:23.170
going to deploy it not
to the default namespace.

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:24.730
We'll do it to a
separate namespace.

00:39:24.730 --> 00:39:26.650
And we'll try things out
there before we roll it out

00:39:26.650 --> 00:39:28.630
to default or to other
ones, where you might

00:39:28.630 --> 00:39:31.940
have production workloads.

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:34.910
Some light reading
to get you started.

00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:38.470
Megan actually, who was the
previous speaker in this room,

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:39.322
gave--

00:39:39.322 --> 00:39:41.780
both of us worked together on
a series of blog posts called

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:42.980
the Service Mesh Era.

00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:45.140
And they're good
conceptual overviews,

00:39:45.140 --> 00:39:47.720
but they've actually
got practical tutorials

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:48.980
built in to each one of those.

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:50.480
And they go through
a lot more depth

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:53.790
here around traffic
splitting, canary rollouts,

00:39:53.790 --> 00:39:56.750
telemetry stuff, the
security one as well.

00:39:56.750 --> 00:39:58.580
And actually, the
last one in the series

00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:01.160
focuses on some more
advanced topics in Istio,

00:40:01.160 --> 00:40:05.090
like mesh expansion,
adding in VMs, right?

00:40:05.090 --> 00:40:07.400
We have a lot of workloads
that still exist on VMs.

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:09.300
I know I went very
Kubernetes centric here,

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:11.885
but we want to make VMs
just as easy as we want

00:40:11.885 --> 00:40:13.010
to make it with containers.

00:40:13.010 --> 00:40:16.010
So you can deploy and expand
your mesh now with VMs,

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:17.790
but it's still a
little bit more work.

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:20.280
It's not as easy as just
injecting the sidecar.

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:21.780
But she's got a
great example there.

00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:23.030
And then there's also
examples of running

00:40:23.030 --> 00:40:24.890
Istio in a multi-cluster
deployment,

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:27.390
whether you have a single
control plane in one cluster,

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:29.300
or you have multiple
control planes.

00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:32.088
So you can have either one
or both of those setups.

00:40:32.088 --> 00:40:33.130
So that's a great series.

00:40:33.130 --> 00:40:35.338
I would definitely advise
you to take a look at that.

00:40:35.338 --> 00:40:38.090
And then the traffic portion
of this came from a blog post

00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:40.340
that I wrote a few months
ago called incremental Istio

00:40:40.340 --> 00:40:41.570
with traffic management.

00:40:41.570 --> 00:40:42.862
So that's another good example.

00:40:42.862 --> 00:40:45.960
It goes into a little more depth
of how you could roll it out.

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.170
There's also great
talks on MTLS migration

00:40:48.170 --> 00:40:50.890
and some of the
conceptual things.

00:40:50.890 --> 00:40:53.950
The last two links are links
to my personal GitHub, where

00:40:53.950 --> 00:40:56.230
I have that weather info
app if you want to just have

00:40:56.230 --> 00:40:57.610
a quick little example.

00:40:57.610 --> 00:40:59.350
Again, I like that
one, because it

00:40:59.350 --> 00:41:02.080
requires you to make that
service entry for outbound API

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:02.892
access.

00:41:02.892 --> 00:41:05.350
And it's got a couple other
nice little components in there

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:06.377
to try out.

00:41:06.377 --> 00:41:07.960
And then all the
stuff I went through,

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:11.860
all that YAML that I truncated
in a couple of slides,

00:41:11.860 --> 00:41:15.212
is actually in this repo,
Next 19 Incremental Istio.

00:41:15.212 --> 00:41:17.420
So it's all there if you
want to run through this end

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:20.140
to end and roll out traffic
slowly or roll out some

00:41:20.140 --> 00:41:21.400
of the security stuff slowly.

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:22.900
There's a fully
baked example there.

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:29.832
Before I close out, I do want to
mention a few other talks that

00:41:29.832 --> 00:41:30.790
are happening tomorrow.

00:41:30.790 --> 00:41:32.810
There's a little bit
more 200 and 300 level.

00:41:32.810 --> 00:41:34.395
But I think they're really good.

00:41:34.395 --> 00:41:36.020
Tomorrow morning,
actually, Megan and I

00:41:36.020 --> 00:41:39.140
are going to give a talk on
how to debug your deployments

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:40.790
inside of Istio, right?

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:43.050
So this is an example
of you deployed Istio,

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:45.470
you deployed your services
with the sidecar proxy,

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:47.130
and something broke, right?

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:49.640
And we're going to take you
through three or four examples

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:53.600
of things like how do I debug
traffic deployments or traffic

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:57.537
errors, how do I debug
telemetry, right?

00:41:57.537 --> 00:41:58.870
What if you have custom metrics?

00:41:58.870 --> 00:42:01.200
How do you get those to show
up inside that Prometheus

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:04.160
that you've got as part
of open source Istio?

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:05.942
And the last one
is security one.

00:42:05.942 --> 00:42:07.400
We're going to go
through, I think,

00:42:07.400 --> 00:42:09.560
some work with MTLS
and job tokens as well.

00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:13.712
So that's very much a demo
centric specific exercise

00:42:13.712 --> 00:42:15.670
for debugging or
troubleshooting, I should say.

00:42:15.670 --> 00:42:18.040
But then we've got a
few other ones as well.

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:21.520
Particularly, I mentioned
the adding Istio for VMs

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:22.840
and expanding the service mesh.

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:23.990
So that's tomorrow as well.

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:32.320
Second to last but not least,
we would love your feedback

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:37.530
about how to make a better
service mesh product, right?

00:42:37.530 --> 00:42:39.100
We love the input
from people who

00:42:39.100 --> 00:42:41.290
are using Istio, who are
playing with Istio, who

00:42:41.290 --> 00:42:42.800
are interested in using Istio.

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:43.300
Why?

00:42:43.300 --> 00:42:44.440
What drives that, right?

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:46.715
We want to help improve
as much as we can.

00:42:46.715 --> 00:42:48.340
So this is feedback
that we use to make

00:42:48.340 --> 00:42:50.715
the commercial version better,
as well as the open source

00:42:50.715 --> 00:42:51.410
version.

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:53.040
So by all means, sign up.

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:54.040
Fill out this form.

00:42:54.040 --> 00:42:57.420
We'd love your
feedback and your help.

00:42:57.420 --> 00:42:59.010
And then finally, thank you.

00:42:59.010 --> 00:43:00.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:04.460
